/**

@page page_exahype_tutorials_euler Euler

-   Clone the repository (cmp. page ). Skip this step if you already
    have a  checkout, i.e. if you have completed this step once.

    git clone -b p4 https://gitlab.lrz.de/hpcsoftware/Peano.git

-   Create the build system (cmp. page ). Skip this step if you already
    have a working configure, i.e. if you have completed this step once.

    libtoolize; aclocal; autoconf; autoheader; cp src/config.h.in .;
    automake --add-missing

-   Configure  with the flags `–enable-exahype`,
    `–enable-loadbalancing`, `–enable-blockstructured`:

    ./configure --enable-exahype --enable-loadbalancing
    --enable-blockstructured

    These are the mandatory extensions (toolboxes). You might want to
    add further options. You also will have to add additional flags on
    particular system. In particular the flags `CXX`, and `LDFLAGS` to
    pick your compiler, your compiler settings and your linker
    parameters. Chapter
    [\[chapter:selected-HPC-platforms\]](#chapter:selected-HPC-platforms){reference-type="ref"
    reference="chapter:selected-HPC-platforms"} discusses some popular
    choices. Skip this step if you have already completed it once.

-   Recompile your  code by typing in

    make

    This should give you a couple of static libraries in the respective
    subdirectories. Skip this step if you have already completed it
    once.

-   Ensure that `python` subdirectory is in your Python search path and

-   run the Jupyter notebook in `examples/exahype2/euler`:

    export PYTHONPATH=../../../python/ jupyter-notebook Euler.ipynb

    or---if you don't like Jupyter notebooks---type in

    export PYTHONPATH=../../../python/ python3
    example-scripts/finitevolumes.py -cs 0.2 ./peano4

-   Finally, jump straight into Chapter
    [\[chapter:postprocessing\]](#chapter:postprocessing){reference-type="ref"
    reference="chapter:postprocessing"} and pick one of the pathways
    there to convert the output files into vtk files which Paraview or
    VisIt can display.

Case study: minimalist Finite Volumes for the Euler equations {#section:exahype:fv}
-------------------------------------------------------------

In the `examples/exahype2` directory, there are multiple  experiments.
An  project is mainly a Python script supplemented by some C++ user
code. We ship of these scripts as Jupyter notebooks. If you prefer to
run the Jupyter notebooks without the GUI, use `ipython3`.

A minimalist  solver resembles:

1.  We first create a new project. This is an  project which is, for the
    time being, completely independent of a  project.

2.  We next create a solver. There's a number of solvers currently
    available within . Once created, we add it to the  project before

3.  we set the global simulation parameters.

4.  As  has already been installed on the system, the developer did set
    all pathes, all compilers, all libraries, ...It would be
    inconvenient to ask a user to provide this information all over
    again. Therefore, we parse the outcome of `configure` and copy the
    relevant information over.

5.  Finally, we ask the  project to generate a new  project.

Once we have a  project, we can add further features to this one.
Eventually, we make the  project generate the actual C++ code.

##### Created code.

The above snippet does set up a complete  simulation. The solver will
consist of two classes: `AbstractEuler` and a subclass `Euler`. The
`Euler` is an empty stub when you call the script the first time. You
will have to befill it with the actual Physics---flux functions for
example. See the files in the directory `examples/exahype2/euler` which
hosts such example code. If you want to avoid to write C++ code at all,
you can also use some symbolic Python interface. An example for this one
is provided via the Jupyter notebook.

##### Changing simulation properties.

's Python API is written as end-to-end interface: Whenever you change
something, you have to run the whole script or Jupyter notebook all over
again. In practice, this might be too much. Often, it is simply
sufficient to repeat the following steps:

-   Change something within your  project (such as the simulation
    runtime, or mesh size, or ...).

-   Regenerate the resulting  project:

-   Re-add all the stuff you had added to the  project before,
    i.e. additional libraries, additional compile flags, additional
    Fortran modules, ...Also set all the constants within the  project
    that have to be exported into C++ code.

